WEBVTT

00:01.500 --> 00:05.500
Every frame has a deadline.

00:06.720 --> 00:09.560
Every five seconds, this satellite takes a picture.

00:09.560 --> 00:12.720
It has forty milliseconds to understand it.

00:12.720 --> 00:15.560
Power, memory, heat, time.

00:15.560 --> 00:18.360
All limited, all changing.

00:18.360 --> 00:21.680
Most adaptive AI picks its model from a cost sheet,

00:21.680 --> 00:23.880
profiled on a developer's laptop.

00:23.880 --> 00:28.860
On a Raspberry Pi, the board a research CubeSat is flying, that sheet was wrong:

00:28.860 --> 00:31.860
six and a half times wrong.

00:31.860 --> 00:37.340
Existing systems adapt to one or two signals: a deadline, the input, or the battery.

00:37.340 --> 00:42.000
MIZAN adapts to all four, and corrects its cost sheet on the board that runs it.

00:42.000 --> 00:44.500
MIZAN, Arabic for balance.

00:44.500 --> 00:50.000
Every frame, it weighs three levers: how deep to think,

00:50.000 --> 00:53.000
whether to look at all, and how much detail.

00:53.000 --> 00:56.000
The network is a small MobileNet with early exits.

00:56.000 --> 00:59.000
When it is confident, it stops early.

00:59.000 --> 01:01.000
When it is not, it goes deeper.

01:01.000 --> 01:04.000
Each frame, the controller prices quality against energy.

01:04.000 --> 01:08.400
A safety filter can veto its choice, but never makes one.

01:08.400 --> 01:13.240
Then the board measures what the frame really cost, and corrects the menu.

01:13.240 --> 01:18.500
We tested it in a mission twin: a simulated orbit, 500 kilometres up,

01:18.500 --> 01:22.560
and over 1,500 decisions, three things go wrong.

01:22.560 --> 01:27.000
For nine minutes, the satellite is in view of a ground station.

01:28.000 --> 01:31.000
The downlink takes the processor, and nobody tells the AI.

01:31.000 --> 01:34.000
The sheet says six milliseconds.

01:34.000 --> 01:37.500
MIZAN expects thirty-nine, and steps down.

01:37.500 --> 01:41.000
Trust the sheet, and you miss sixty-four deadlines.

01:41.000 --> 01:46.000
MIZAN misses only twelve.

01:46.000 --> 01:49.500
Then the payload overheats. MIZAN pauses until it cools,

01:49.500 --> 01:54.560
safe, but it skips images a lighter model could have taken.

01:54.560 --> 01:58.000
Eclipse, with one solar wing stuck.

01:58.000 --> 02:02.000
It narrows its choices, rests, and recovers on its own.

02:02.000 --> 02:05.000
And here is the surprise.

02:05.000 --> 02:10.000
On a data-centre chip running flat out, early exit saves about half the energy.

02:10.000 --> 02:14.000
At a payload's pace, nothing measurable.

02:14.000 --> 02:18.000
Adapting the model buys deadline safety, not energy.

02:18.000 --> 02:21.000
The energy lever is the power switch.

02:21.000 --> 02:24.000
On the real Raspberry Pi,

02:24.000 --> 02:27.000
cost feedback cut missed deadlines nearly in half.

02:27.000 --> 02:33.000
Early exit ran more than twice as fast as full depth, for under one point of accuracy,

02:33.000 --> 02:39.000
and the oscilloscope shows that full depth cost about three times the energy of an early exit.

02:39.000 --> 02:45.220
Then we ran the whole system live on the board: 1,440 frames.

02:45.220 --> 02:50.620
Its latency forecast held, wrong on no more than 5% of frames, as designed.

02:50.620 --> 02:55.300
It was more accurate than the cheapest option, though not yet significantly,

02:55.300 --> 03:01.700
but it missed ten deadlines where the cheapest missed none: a cold start, before it had measured enough.

03:01.700 --> 03:07.000
The fix, still to test: start from the board's own samples.

03:07.000 --> 03:09.000
We even killed it mid-mission.

03:09.000 --> 03:14.000
Ten seconds later, it was back, and nothing recorded was lost.

03:14.000 --> 03:19.000
Held out and pre-registered, we ran it once. It caused no breach in a hundred runs.

03:19.000 --> 03:24.000
Where deadlines or memory move, it beats every fixed option.

03:24.000 --> 03:27.560
Under heat, it paused when it could have stepped down.

03:27.560 --> 03:34.000
A step-down rule, written afterwards, beats every single fixed option in simulation.

03:34.000 --> 03:39.400
Onboard, adaptive inference must measure its platform, and keep measuring it.

03:39.400 --> 03:43.600
MIZAN. Measure the board, then decide.
